## 1. Constructor Overloading with Name and Age

### Concept

Constructor overloading means creating multiple constructors in the same class with different parameter lists.

```java
class Person {
    String name;
    int age;

    Person(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }

    Person(String name, int age) {
        this.name = name;
        this.age = age;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Person p1 = new Person("Niks");
        Person p2 = new Person("Siri", 21);

        System.out.println(p1.name);
        System.out.println(p2.name + " " + p2.age);
    }
}
```

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

**Output**

```text
Niks
Siri 21
```

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

**Explanation**

- `Person(String name)` accepts one argument.
- `Person(String name, int age)` accepts two arguments.
- Java selects the matching constructor based on the arguments supplied.

**Dry run**

- `new Person("Niks")` calls the one-parameter constructor.
- `new Person("Siri", 21)` calls the two-parameter constructor.

**Complexity:** Time \(O(1)\), auxiliary space \(O(1)\) per constructor call.

