## 2. Constructor Overloading with Different Data Types

### Concept

Constructors can be overloaded using different parameter counts or types.

```java
class Product {
    String name;
    double price;
    int quantity;

    Product(String name, double price) {
        this.name = name;
        this.price = price;
    }

    Product(String name, double price, int quantity) {
        this.name = name;
        this.price = price;
        this.quantity = quantity;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Product p1 = new Product("Laptop", 50000.0);
        Product p2 = new Product("Phone", 20000.0, 2);

        System.out.println(p1.name + " " + p1.price);
        System.out.println(p2.name + " " + p2.price + " " + p2.quantity);
    }
}
```

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

**Output**

```text
Laptop 50000.0
Phone 20000.0 2
```

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

**Explanation**

The first constructor accepts two arguments. The second accepts three.

**Interview tip:** The parameter list determines which overloaded constructor is selected. Return type is not part of a constructor signature.

**Complexity:** Time \(O(1)\), auxiliary space \(O(1)\) per object creation.

