## 3. Constructor Overloading with Different Parameter Order

### Concept

Parameter order can distinguish overloaded constructors when the parameter types appear in a different order.

```java
class Employee {
    String name;
    int id;

    Employee(String name, int id) {
        this.name = name;
        this.id = id;
    }

    Employee(int id, String name) {
        this.name = name;
        this.id = id;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Employee e1 = new Employee("Rahul", 101);
        Employee e2 = new Employee(102, "Priya");

        System.out.println(e1.name + " " + e1.id);
        System.out.println(e2.name + " " + e2.id);
    }
}
```

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

**Output**

```text
Rahul 101
Priya 102
```

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

**Important:** Changing only parameter names does not create a new overloaded constructor. The parameter types, number, or order must distinguish the signatures.

**Complexity:** Time \(O(1)\), auxiliary space \(O(1)\) per object creation.

