## 4. Constructor Overloading with Default Values

### Concept

A constructor can supply a default value when the caller does not provide one.

```java
class Book {
    String title;
    double price;

    Book(String title, double price) {
        this.title = title;
        this.price = price;
    }

    Book(String title) {
        this(title, 0.0);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Book b1 = new Book("Java", 450.0);
        Book b2 = new Book("DSA");

        System.out.println(b1.title + " " + b1.price);
        System.out.println(b2.title + " " + b2.price);
    }
}
```

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

**Output**

```text
Java 450.0
DSA 0.0
```

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

**Explanation**

`this(title, 0.0)` calls the two-parameter constructor and supplies `0.0` as the default price.

**Memory trick:** Missing value? Use constructor chaining to supply a default.

**Complexity:** Time \(O(1)\), auxiliary space \(O(1)\) per object creation.

