## 8. Employee Constructor Chaining

### Concept

Use constructor chaining to supply a default employee name and salary.

```java
class Employee {
    String name;
    int salary;

    Employee() {
        this("Not Assigned");
    }

    Employee(String name) {
        this(name, 20000);
    }

    Employee(String name, int salary) {
        this.name = name;
        this.salary = salary;

        System.out.println("Name: " + this.name);
        System.out.println("Salary: " + this.salary);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Employee e1 = new Employee();
        Employee e2 = new Employee("Rahul", 35000);
    }
}
```

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

**Output**

```text
Name: Not Assigned
Salary: 20000
Name: Rahul
Salary: 35000
```

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

**Explanation**

- `Employee()` delegates to `Employee(String)`.
- `Employee(String)` delegates to `Employee(String, int)`.
- The two-parameter constructor stores and prints the values.

**Common mistake:** Writing `this("Not Assigned", 20000)` when no matching two-parameter constructor exists causes a compilation error.

**Complexity:** Time \(O(1)\), auxiliary space \(O(1)\) per object creation.

